WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:37.010
So today I’m gonna tell you the best three
stories that I know, I think, and, I think

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:44.899
these stories are absolutely, phenomenally
potent, and the reason for that is that I

00:00:44.899 --> 00:00:53.940
think they illustrate in an extraordinarily-powerful
way the nature of the processes that led to

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:58.510
the establishment of western democratic ideals.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:08.500
Mircae Eliade, whose work I rely on substantially,
to make the following argument, has taken

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:14.479
western academic culture to task in a manner
that hasn’t fully been revealed yet.

00:01:14.479 --> 00:01:20.890
He said that, like all human cultures, the
west is parochial and narrow in its viewpoint,

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:25.310
and although over the last three hundred years
there’s been a substantial amount of cross-cultural

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:30.490
intermingling and a potential broadening of
philosophical and religious horizon, that

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:39.910
broadening has not been sufficiently complete;

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:45.179
If we build on the viewpoint that I’ve been
developing with you over the last few courses

00:01:45.179 --> 00:01:49.320
and make the presupposition that religious
modes of thinking are more phenomenological

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.280
than they are rational, which means that they
deal more with what you experience, say, than

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:59.000
with the objective world, if we look at archaic
religious stories through that lens, then

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:04.399
they can start to open up, and so, given that,
I want to open up three religious stories

00:02:04.399 --> 00:02:13.750
to you today, two in depth, and one only in
passing because we’ll return to it later.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:22.190
I’ll start with, Judeo-Christian Myth of
Genesis, and when I say myth, of course, you

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:25.739
all know this already, but when I say myth,
I don’t mean untruth, I mean a form of knowledge

00:02:25.739 --> 00:02:30.569
that’s narrative in structure and predicated
on presumptions that aren’t empirical; a

00:02:30.569 --> 00:02:35.310
myth describes processes of transformation,
a myth describes them, a myth describes the

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:38.680
process whereby elements of experience come
into being and transform

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:51.260
On Genesis, the fundamental structural elements
of Genesis are the Word of God and chaos,

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:58.269
and the Hebrew words for chaos and waste are
tehom, for chaos, and tohu, for waste, and

00:02:58.269 --> 00:03:03.269
tehom in particular, although toho as well,
are very interesting words because you can

00:03:03.269 --> 00:03:08.980
track their derivation historically, and sometimes
if you contract the derivation of words historically,

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:13.659
you can get some sense of the culture milieu
out of which the word sprang,

00:03:13.659 --> 00:03:19.249
and we know what tyomat means because we have
written records of a story that involves a

00:03:19.249 --> 00:03:24.761
character named Tiamat, and Tiamat is a dragon
who lives at the bottom of the ocean; in the

00:03:24.761 --> 00:03:31.609
oldest creation myth we have, which is the
Enuma Elish, the Sumerian creation myth, the

00:03:31.609 --> 00:03:44.629
Sumeria, Babylon, Acadia, rightly regarded
as the birthplace of modern western civilization.

00:03:44.629 --> 00:03:54.950
In Genesis, two processes unite to produce
being, the first of those processes or states

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:59.769
is chaos, represented by taom or tohu.

00:03:59.769 --> 00:04:05.430
The second of those processes is logos, and
logos is another word that has an extraordinarily

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:13.200
broad range of meaning; it’s generally transcribed
in the Christian tradition as the word, and

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:17.820
it’s identified with Christ, which is a
very peculiar identification because it’s

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:23.120
the Word of God that creates order out of
chaos and, of course, the Word of God in Genesis

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:28.480
is a phenomena that predates the birth of
Christ infinitely from a classical religious

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:36.740
perspective, so the fact that the two beings
are identified as of great peculiarity and

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:39.280
also of great interest.

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:45.340
The fundamental story of Genesis is something
like this, and it’s perhaps the most brilliant

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:53.800
contribution of Judeo-Christian thought to
world history, its total impact is virtually

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:55.770
incalculable.

00:04:55.770 --> 00:05:00.690
The idea is this: that chaos can be conceptualized
as something that has an essentially feminine

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:07.270
aspect as a matrix, and a matrix is a structure
from which other structures emerge, and the

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:12.980
story in Genesis makes the hypothesis that
logos, which is the Word of God, a phenomena

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:22.349
associated with speech and communication and
logic, logos: logic, rationality, courage,

00:05:22.349 --> 00:05:28.730
exploration, all combined into a single entity
or trait: logos, the combination of logos

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:33.080
and chaos is what brings order into being;
that’s what the story in Genesis means;

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:39.960
it’s not an empirical description of the
origin of objects, it’s a phenomenological

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:47.080
description of the origin of experience,
the idea being that without the piercing glance

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:55.370
of whatever consciousness is, whatever the
background of experience is, the matrix, chaos,

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:59.810
cannot be conceptualized as real; it takes
the interplay between the feminine principle,

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:06.099
chaos, and the masculine principle, logos,
in order to produce being.

00:06:06.099 --> 00:06:10.840
Now that’s of substantial importance when
you give some consideration to the fact that

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:21.760
immediately in Genesis, after the establishment
of livable order, the deity yahva identifies

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:26.819
the individual human being with logos, right,
made in God’s image, that’s the essential

00:06:26.819 --> 00:06:32.259
characteristic of the human being, and what
that means is that the logos that operates

00:06:32.259 --> 00:06:36.569
in human beings, which is this capacity to
make order out of chaos, is identical to the

00:06:36.569 --> 00:06:41.810
principle that gives rise to the cosmos from
a mythological perspective, so it partakes

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:50.849
of the deity in a very direct sense insofar
as being itself is dependent on its operation.

00:06:50.849 --> 00:06:57.419
Now it’s still possible to claim, given
that perspective, that a story with that sort

00:06:57.419 --> 00:07:02.349
of structure is superstitious and that it
doesn’t bear any relationship to what reality,

00:07:02.349 --> 00:07:07.060
what actually constitutes reality, but you
have to understand that that story, old as

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:12.590
it is, is predicated on older stories, and
it’s on the ground that those older stories

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:18.970
establish that our entire concept of natural
rights rests, so if you believe that natural

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:25.430
rights have an existence more than merely
arbitrary, the reason you believe that is

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:28.830
because they’re predicated, those rights
are predicated on the ideas that are expressed

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:32.530
in these myths,

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:39.039
Okay, so now we’re gonna go back in time
to Sumeria, and I’m gonna show you how the

00:07:39.039 --> 00:07:45.009
Sumerian creation myth lays itself out, and
not only that, I’m going to describe to

00:07:45.009 --> 00:07:50.319
you the direct political implications of the
Enuma Elish, the Sumerian creation myth, because

00:07:50.319 --> 00:07:55.210
the political implications of that myth are
well understood because the political structure

00:07:55.210 --> 00:08:01.180
of Sumeria was directly associated with the
structure of the myth because the Sumeria

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:08.509
was regarded as the earthly represent of the
highest God in the Mesopotamian Pantheon,

00:08:08.509 --> 00:08:13.600
whose name was Marduk, so insofar as you were
emperor, the reason that you were emperor,

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:20.219
and the, this is what gave your sovereignty
legitimacy, right, ‘cause sovereignty has

00:08:20.219 --> 00:08:23.499
to have legitimacy otherwise there’s constant
revolutions.

00:08:23.499 --> 00:08:28.250
What gave sovereignty legitimacy in Mesopotamia
was the identification of the emperor with

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:32.099
Marduk, and that had, that had certain implications
for the emperor, which we’ll discuss in

00:08:32.099 --> 00:08:33.099
some detail

00:08:33.099 --> 00:08:34.860
So the story starts like this.

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:46.730
You’ve got this Dragon, Tiamat, and Tiamat
is a great primordial beast, who lives at

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:48.890
the bottom of the ocean.

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:54.370
And the ocean is water and water is associated
with the primal element in archaic thinking

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:56.540
and I told you, there’s reasons for that
already.

00:08:56.540 --> 00:09:01.270
or if you viewed the transformation of deserts
as a consequence of rain, you can understand

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:05.770
why water would be considered the element
that gives life, right, that the element that,

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:09.860
that brings life forth, and we know from an
evolutionary perspective that that’s accurate.

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:14.220
And we know that we’re 90 per cent water
and so to consider water the primal element

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:16.760
is no trivial conclusion.

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:21.880
No, no less than presuming that the sun is
the ultimate source of light, the ultimate

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:25.130
god, because the sun is the ultimate source
of life as far as earth, as far as we’re

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:27.920
concerned on earth, right, because it’s
the source of all our energy.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:29.760
These aren’t stupid concepts, all right.

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:35.770
So Tiamat is this horrible creature that lurks
at the bottom of the primal element, all right.

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:37.650
Now she has a husband, Apsu.

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:41.390
Now the Mesopotamian creation myth doesn’t
say much about Apsu.

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:44.401
We only know that he’s the male consort
of the Dragon of Chaos.

00:09:44.401 --> 00:09:50.870
And we know from reading other sources of
mythology that the male consort of the Dragon

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:54.890
of Chaos generally represents either logos
or culture.

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:59.980
So we’re gonna make the presupposition in
this particular case that the husband of Chaos

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:05.480
is Order or Culture, okay, and then the Mesopotamians
don’t say much about that, that doesn’t,

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:11.940
the development of the idea of Apsu or Order
or Culture, doesn’t take new force until

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:14.230
the ancient Egyptians and we’ll talk about
them today too.

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:20.881
Okay so Tiamat and Apsu are locked into a
kind of sexual embrace according to the Mesopotamian

00:10:20.881 --> 00:10:21.980
creation.

00:10:21.980 --> 00:10:23.230
What does that mean?

00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:24.310
It kinda means two things.

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:29.290
It means that they’re not really distinguishable
because they’re locked into this embrace

00:10:29.290 --> 00:10:34.580
and it also means that they’re up to something
creative because the act of sexual congress

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:41.910
in mythology is most usefully, is most frequently
utilized as representation of something creative

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:47.940
or as representation of the probability of
some new form coming to be, right.

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:49.290
All right.

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:57.700
So Apsu and Tiamat are locked into this embrace
in a state that other creation myths describe

00:10:57.700 --> 00:11:06.250
as egg-like, the Pre-Cosmogonic egg, their
intermingling gives rise to the initial state

00:11:06.250 --> 00:11:11.700
of being according to the Sumerians and the
initial state of being according to the Sumerians

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:15.200
is characterized by the dominion of the elder
gods

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:23.450
These gods, being none too bright, make a
tremendous amount of racket, doing things.

00:11:23.450 --> 00:11:27.540
Well what does that mean exactly?

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:31.070
Well it means something like this.

00:11:31.070 --> 00:11:33.170
They make a lot of racket and they cause a
lot of trouble.

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:40.410
They make a lot of wind and all of their racket
and trouble and wind and activity rouses Tiamat.

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:44.020
What does that mean?

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:46.860
Well it means if you do things, you get in
trouble.

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:50.320
It means even if you’re trying to solve
problems, you get into trouble because the

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:54.300
solution to a problem tend, tends to generate
a whole bunch of new problems, right.

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:55.690
It’s like the Hydra.

00:11:55.690 --> 00:12:06.000
So what it means is that it’s more or less
fated that any form of activity whatsoever

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:11.050
is likely to produce the threat of catastrophe

00:12:11.050 --> 00:12:15.490
And of course we’re absolutely keenly aware
of that in the modern world because we’re

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:21.970
possessed by this sense that all of our frenetic
activity, all of our frenetic motivated activity

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:54.360
is producing alterations in the world order
such that Nature itself is going to 

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:58.180
be destroyed and eliminate us, right.

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:00.350
That’s classic Sumerian fear.

00:13:00.350 --> 00:13:03.390
Nothing’s changed in the last 5,000 years.

00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:08.060
The Sumerians presumed that once the elder
gods where constructed and started moving

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:13.720
around on the planet, that their activity
their mindless activity, because remember

00:13:13.720 --> 00:13:18.300
these aren’t well integrated motivational
forces, they’re more like primordial beasts,

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:19.300
right.

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:25.590
Their unintegrated activity risks plunging
everything back in Chaos.

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:30.230
Well Tiamat’s the representative of Chaos,
this generative Chaos, but so what if the

00:13:30.230 --> 00:13:35.490
Sumerians say, “Well the elder gods cause
a lot of racket, they move around the planet

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:41.760
and they upset Tiamat,” and she decides
that enough is enough and she’s gonna wipe

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:43.640
them out.

00:13:43.640 --> 00:13:51.890
So she’s sitting at the bottom of the ocean,
fuming away, as the elder gods go about their

00:13:51.890 --> 00:13:58.530
business and then they take one step too many
and they kill Apsu, who’s her husband.

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:03.060
Now the Sumerian creation myth doesn’t say
much about this, but we know that Apsu is

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:04.840
the male consort of Chaos.

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:06.130
That makes him Order.

00:14:06.130 --> 00:14:10.520
So what happens is the elder gods destroy
Order itself.

00:14:10.520 --> 00:14:13.930
They destroy Culture itself and as soon as
you destroy Culture, all hell breaks loose

00:14:13.930 --> 00:14:16.221
and that’s exactly what happens in this
Sumerian creation myth.

00:14:16.221 --> 00:14:23.450
So Tiamat emerges, he’s gonna wipe everything
out and the world will revert back to its

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:26.050
primordial, non-existent state.

00:14:26.050 --> 00:14:29.440
Now the, all the gods get wind of this and
of course they’re just terrified because

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:34.380
they know that this thing that gave rise to
them, whatever it is, the matrix of being,

00:14:34.380 --> 00:14:38.850
can easily wipe them out at a moment’s notice
and so despite the fact that they are transpersonal

00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:44.120
and immortal and characterized by a certain
amount of power, in the face of absolute Chaos,

00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:45.800
they’re insufficient.

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:52.310
Now this occurrence is extending over a protracted
period of time and as the elder gods are threatened,

00:14:52.310 --> 00:14:55.820
they’re also breeding and mating and producing
new forms.

00:14:55.820 --> 00:14:59.610
And they produce a great, great grandson,
whose name is Marduk.

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:02.180
Now Marduk has a lot of very interesting attributes.

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:06.190
So the attributes are described in the Enuma
Elish in the following manner, so this is

00:15:06.190 --> 00:15:12.100
what Marduk’s father sees when his wife,
Damkina, gives birth to Marduk.

00:15:12.100 --> 00:15:17.620
“So when he, who’s Marduk’s father,
saw his son, he rejoiced, he beamed, his heart

00:15:17.620 --> 00:15:19.680
was filled with joy.

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:24.560
He distinguished him and conferred upon him
double equality with the gods.”

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:28.870
Okay, so that’s that first indication that
whatever Marduk represents is something that’s

00:15:28.870 --> 00:15:32.500
elevated beyond the normal status of a primordial
deity.

00:15:32.500 --> 00:15:36.930
“So that he was highly exalted and surpassed
them in everything.

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:41.980
Artfully arranged beyond comprehension were
his members, not fit for human understanding,

00:15:41.980 --> 00:15:46.310
hard to look upon, four were his eyes, four
were his ears.

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:48.890
When his lips moved, fired blazed forth.

00:15:48.890 --> 00:15:53.050
Each of his four ears grew large and likewise,
his eyes, to see everything.

00:15:53.050 --> 00:15:55.550
He was exalted among gods.

00:15:55.550 --> 00:15:56.930
Surpassing was his form.

00:15:56.930 --> 00:15:58.600
His members were gigantic.

00:15:58.600 --> 00:16:00.220
He was surpassing in height.

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:01.410
Mariyutu, Mariyutu.”

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:02.920
One of his names.

00:16:02.920 --> 00:16:07.130
“Sun of the sun god, the sun god of the
gods.”

00:16:07.130 --> 00:16:08.720
Okay.

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:10.230
Complex bit of poetry.

00:16:10.230 --> 00:16:11.230
Says a bunch of things.

00:16:11.230 --> 00:16:17.170
It says well whatever Marduk is, is the offspring
of the gods.

00:16:17.170 --> 00:16:19.980
Whatever Marduk is, is characterized by heightened
awareness.

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:20.980
Right?

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:23.250
Because he has four ears and they’re large.

00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:29.240
Whatever Marduk is characterized by is the
status that surpasses that of his fathers.

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:34.640
Whatever he’s characterized by, is associated
with the power of speech, real power, because

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:37.570
when he speaks, fire spurts forth.

00:16:37.570 --> 00:16:41.910
Marduk is also huge, but more importantly,
he’s associated in this particular poem

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:43.420
with the sun.

00:16:43.420 --> 00:16:44.510
Why is that?

00:16:44.510 --> 00:16:48.720
Well the sun dominates consciousness, right,
because we’re conscious during the day.

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:52.780
Most of our brain is visual cortex, so we’re
visual creatures.

00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:56.180
So when the sun rises is when the day begins.

00:16:56.180 --> 00:17:00.970
So Marduk is also associated with whatever
deity dominates the day and that’s the deity

00:17:00.970 --> 00:17:02.050
of consciousness.

00:17:02.050 --> 00:17:05.890
And there’s more to the story of the Sun,
right, because the Sun is also something that

00:17:05.890 --> 00:17:11.569
rises and sets repeatedly and that means that
the deity that dominates consciousness is

00:17:11.569 --> 00:17:13.949
characterized by a cyclical nature.

00:17:13.949 --> 00:17:15.620
That’s a Sun myth.

00:17:15.620 --> 00:17:20.799
Sun rises in the morning renewed, as a consequence
of fighting a terrible battle in the night

00:17:20.799 --> 00:17:24.059
with the enemies of everything that’s associated
with consciousness.

00:17:24.059 --> 00:17:30.740
A classic solar myth

00:17:30.740 --> 00:17:36.000
So Apsu and Tiamat give rise to the world
of the gods.

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:42.420
The activity of the world of gods reawakens
Tiamat, she decides to destroy everything,

00:17:42.420 --> 00:17:46.550
but at that moment the gods give birth to
Marduk.

00:17:46.550 --> 00:17:51.130
Now that’s a typical motif which is that
the hero’s always born at the time of maximal

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:54.830
crisis and the reason for that is in part,
it’s simple.

00:17:54.830 --> 00:18:00.100
Look, if your culture is dealing well with
the forces of the unknown, so that everything

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:05.770
is static, but, static but productive, so
that problems themselves don’t arise, there’s

00:18:05.770 --> 00:18:08.830
no reason for the hero, right, there’s no
reason to confront the unknown.

00:18:08.830 --> 00:18:13.770
It’s only when crisis beckons that the birth
of the hero is necessary.

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:18.140
Marduk constitutes the birth of the hero.

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:21.350
and they say, “Look, you know, we’re in
real trouble unless someone goes out there

00:18:21.350 --> 00:18:28.600
and confronts Tiamat straight on, she’s
gonna wipe everything out, it’s a dangerous

00:18:28.600 --> 00:18:31.050
and terrifying job, but somebody has to do
it.

00:18:31.050 --> 00:18:34.760
And Marduk says, “Oh that’s no problem
but I got a few rules here and this is the

00:18:34.760 --> 00:18:40.530
first rule, I’m in charge from here on forward.”

00:18:40.530 --> 00:18:43.170
What does that mean exactly?

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:51.930
Well these archaic stories are polysemus and,
or polysemic in Northrup Frye’s terminology

00:18:51.930 --> 00:18:58.030
and what that means is that they can be read
validly at multiple levels of analysis simultaneously.

00:18:58.030 --> 00:19:03.960
And so one thing it means is that if you take
the 2-year-old child who’s essentially under

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:08.140
the Dominion of assorted primordial gods and
goddesses.

00:19:08.140 --> 00:19:09.230
Right?

00:19:09.230 --> 00:19:18.310
Aggression, fear, panic, and according to
Freud, a certain degree of sexual aggression.

00:19:18.310 --> 00:19:25.120
The child moves from motivated state to domination
for motivated state It isn’t until the age

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:30.880
of 3 and 4 when, under the pressure applied
by the social world and as a consequence of

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:36.300
the maturation of the prefrontal cortex, which
matures throughout childhood and adolescence

00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:41.820
and doesn’t reach its final form until perhaps
into the early 20s, all of those fundamental

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:47.660
motivational forces start taking on a structured
relationship to one another, which is to say

00:19:47.660 --> 00:19:56.390
that once your, as your personality becomes
integrated, a single motive force forward

00:19:56.390 --> 00:20:01.380
has to bring all of these underlying motivational
systems into some sort of harmonious arrangement.

00:20:01.380 --> 00:20:04.380
Okay so let’s, let’s see what the Sumerians
are doing here.

00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:06.490
Okay, first of all they’re, they’re doing
psychology.

00:20:06.490 --> 00:20:12.350
They’re trying to figure out, given the
dominion of the, the elder gods, the indisputable

00:20:12.350 --> 00:20:16.700
dominion of the elder gods, Instincts, who
should rule?

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:17.700
Right?

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:19.130
What should be in charge?

00:20:19.130 --> 00:20:21.740
How do you construct a hierarchy of values?

00:20:21.740 --> 00:20:28.410
And then more complexly, when you integrate
a state, which is what the Mesopotamians did,

00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:31.610
right, the first great civilization, what
does that mean?

00:20:31.610 --> 00:20:39.130
The first time hundreds of tribes were hammered
into some sort of stable, hierarchical order,

00:20:39.130 --> 00:20:43.309
how do you represent that order?

00:20:43.309 --> 00:20:50.000
“So Marduk gets his act together, the gods
all meet in a huge chamber, they elect him

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:52.570
king and then they prepare him for battle.

00:20:52.570 --> 00:20:58.590
When the gods, the fathers, beheld the power
of his word, they were glad and did homage,

00:20:58.590 --> 00:21:00.100
saying ‘Marduk is king’.

00:21:00.100 --> 00:21:03.850
They bestowed upon him a scepter, a throne
and a royal robe.

00:21:03.850 --> 00:21:08.530
They gave him an irresistable weapon, smiting
the enemy saying, ‘Go and cut off the life

00:21:08.530 --> 00:21:09.530
of Tiamat.

00:21:09.530 --> 00:21:13.260
May the winds carry her blood to out-of-the-way
places.’

00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:17.530
After the gods, his fathers, had determined
the destiny of Marduk, they set him on the

00:21:17.530 --> 00:21:21.940
road, the way to success and attainment.”

00:21:21.940 --> 00:21:28.020
So then he goes to the “heart of darkness”
so to speak and confronts Tiamat, accuses

00:21:28.020 --> 00:21:31.720
her of treachery and challenges her to battle.

00:21:31.720 --> 00:21:36.330
“When Tiamat heard this, she became like
one in a frenzy and lost her reason.

00:21:36.330 --> 00:21:38.180
She cried out loud and furiously.

00:21:38.180 --> 00:21:41.280
To the very roots, her two legs shook back
and forth.

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:45.170
She recited an incantation, repeatedly casting
her spell.

00:21:45.170 --> 00:21:48.010
As for the gods of battle, they sharpened
their weapons.

00:21:48.010 --> 00:21:52.730
Tiamat and Marduk, the wisest of the gods,
advanced against one another.

00:21:52.730 --> 00:21:56.440
They pressed on into combat and approached
for battle.”

00:21:56.440 --> 00:22:01.600
Okay, well things don’t go out so well with
Tiamat from this point forward.

00:22:01.600 --> 00:22:06.990
The first thing that Marduk does is encapsulate
her in a net and I think that’s a really

00:22:06.990 --> 00:22:11.420
interesting metaphor because that’s essentially
what human beings do when they encounter the

00:22:11.420 --> 00:22:12.420
unknown, right.

00:22:12.420 --> 00:22:19.480
They encapsulate it in an explanatory network,
so it’s a way of binding up the, the anomaly,

00:22:19.480 --> 00:22:23.570
or the unknown, and giving it a substantive
form.

00:22:23.570 --> 00:22:29.420
“Then he cuts her into pieces and then he
makes the world out of her pieces.

00:22:29.420 --> 00:22:36.180
In fact one of Marduk’s names is ‘He Who
Makes Ingenious Things’ as a consequence

00:22:36.180 --> 00:22:37.920
of the combat with Tiamat.”

00:22:37.920 --> 00:22:43.510
Now that’s very, very interesting because
what it means is that the Sumerians are presenting

00:22:43.510 --> 00:22:49.270
in metaphorical form the notion that when
the chips are down, the survival of being

00:22:49.270 --> 00:22:54.580
depends on the capacity of whatever Marduk
represents, the solar god, to encounter the

00:22:54.580 --> 00:22:59.330
matrix of being, to cut it into pieces and
to make the world and if you think about in

00:22:59.330 --> 00:23:01.910
those terms, it’s a very, very straightforward
story, right.

00:23:01.910 --> 00:23:06.521
It’s basically the story of human beings,
fundamentally the story of human beings, because

00:23:06.521 --> 00:23:12.760
we, in the words of a famous evolutionary
psychologist, whose name completely escapes

00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:15.620
me, we occupy the cognitive niche, right.

00:23:15.620 --> 00:23:23.830
Our mode of being is creativity in the face
of the unknown and when Chaos threatens the

00:23:23.830 --> 00:23:28.600
established mode of being, it’s necessary
for us to put our creativity into action and

00:23:28.600 --> 00:23:34.190
to carve out new territory as a consequence
of encounter with the unknown.

00:23:34.190 --> 00:23:35.190
Why?

00:23:35.190 --> 00:23:39.520
Well because we can take the world apart with
our hands and put it together in new ways

00:23:39.520 --> 00:23:44.200
and then we can code what we’ve done verbally
and we can transmit it to another person and

00:23:44.200 --> 00:23:45.730
then they can do the same thing.

00:23:45.730 --> 00:23:48.850
And we’re all doing this all the time and
we’re all telling each other how we’re

00:23:48.850 --> 00:23:49.850
doing it.

00:23:49.850 --> 00:23:54.040
And that’s how the embodiment of logos in
the human being, which is precisely equivalent

00:23:54.040 --> 00:23:58.480
to the Sumerian notion of Marduk, that’s
precisely how it is that we’re constantly

00:23:58.480 --> 00:24:02.380
capable of redeeming the world.

00:24:02.380 --> 00:24:10.010
and that’s why you make resolutions at New
Year’s because the new you is supposed to

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:11.640
be born at the new year.

00:24:11.640 --> 00:24:15.100
OK, so what do the
Mesopotamians do at the new year?

00:24:15.100 --> 00:24:18.680
They take their king and they bring him outside
the city.

00:24:18.680 --> 00:24:24.260
Now you have to understand that outside the
city is chaos, right, because these are city

00:24:24.260 --> 00:24:25.700
states.

00:24:25.700 --> 00:24:31.630
When you go outside the dominion of the human,
you’re in chaos.

00:24:31.630 --> 00:24:37.720
And then the priest makes the emperor kneel
and takes all his marks of status off him

00:24:37.720 --> 00:24:44.559
so he’s reduced to his essence fundamentally,
bereft of his social persona, and then he

00:24:44.559 --> 00:24:50.670
slaps him with a glove and humiliateshim and
the king is forced to recount his sins, right,

00:24:50.670 --> 00:24:53.680
everything he did in the
last year that wasn’t up to Marduk standards,

00:24:53.680 --> 00:24:54.680
so to speak.

00:24:54.680 --> 00:24:58.040
And you can see, that’d be a pretty useful
thing to have somebody who’s in power do

00:24:58.040 --> 00:25:03.970
on a regular basis, right, because it reminds
them that they’re in fact subject to a trans-personal

00:25:03.970 --> 00:25:09.890
structure whose nature isn’t precisely evident
but is nonetheless there, which is the case.

00:25:09.890 --> 00:25:14.700
Act like Marduk or all hell will break loose
and demolish your kingdom, which of course

00:25:14.700 --> 00:25:17.260
is true now,
as it was then now.

00:25:17.260 --> 00:25:24.660
So the emperor gets humiliated, he has to
recount his sins, then he’s locked up, then

00:25:24.660 --> 00:25:29.940
he
Re-enacts the battle with Tiamat and when

00:25:29.940 --> 00:25:36.601
he emerges victorious, he’s locked up with
a ritual prostitute, a hierodule, and they

00:25:36.601 --> 00:25:37.601
mate.

00:25:37.601 --> 00:25:38.601
Why?

00:25:38.601 --> 00:25:43.070
Well, the
Hierodule, the ritual prostitute, represents

00:25:43.070 --> 00:25:44.070
Tiamat.

00:25:44.070 --> 00:25:46.550
Now why the hell
would that be, because Tiamat’s a dragon,

00:25:46.550 --> 00:25:51.340
right, a horrible man-eating dragon that lives
at the bottom of the ocean which is to say

00:25:51.340 --> 00:25:57.020
that in any encounter with the
unknown, as difficult traumatic and violent

00:25:57.020 --> 00:26:02.610
as that might be, there’s also the possibility
for something creative to emerge as a consequence,

00:26:02.610 --> 00:26:09.120
right, because it’s out of the
unknown that we mine new information

00:26:09.120 --> 00:26:15.180
So insofar as the Mesopotamian emperor acted
out the role of Marduk, then he was a good

00:26:15.180 --> 00:26:20.540
emperor, then he deserved his sovereignty
and literally as well as figuratively, insofar

00:26:20.540 --> 00:26:24.670
as he did play that role, then the society
was going to remain not only stable but constantly

00:26:24.670 --> 00:26:31.610
updated because he’s engaged in this constant
creative contact with the unknown, aided and

00:26:31.610 --> 00:26:37.340
abetted by his attempts to remember his own
inadequacies and weaknesses and to do something

00:26:37.340 --> 00:26:38.340
about them.

00:26:38.340 --> 00:26:41.910
OK, so that’s a pretty interesting story
and it gets even more interesting when you

00:26:41.910 --> 00:26:48.200
start to understand that the Judao-Christian
myth is assimilated to the Sumerian creation

00:26:48.200 --> 00:26:52.630
myth by the union of the notions of chaos
and Tiamat.

00:26:52.630 --> 00:26:57.170
So the logos in Judao-Christian thinking,
the word of God that produces order out of

00:26:57.170 --> 00:27:03.270
chaos, is also essentially equivalent – at
least metaphorically speaking – to whatever

00:27:03.270 --> 00:27:06.470
Marduk represents in the Sumerian creation
myth.

00:27:06.470 --> 00:27:11.080
And we know that I mean our relationship with
the Enuma Elish is obscured by time but our

00:27:11.080 --> 00:27:15.730
relationship with the stories that lay out
the fundamental sub-structure of western culture

00:27:15.730 --> 00:27:19.850
is not so ameliorated even though we may not
believe them explicitly anymore.

00:27:19.850 --> 00:27:22.070
They still sit at the basis of our society.

